    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay sheds light on the untold narratives of sexual violence in the 1971 Bangladeshi genocide, seeking justice for the victims of sexual violence through the use of their own narratives. In amplifying their voices, the author ascribes to the belief of history as possibility rather than repetition.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ahsan, Z. (2024). An Archive of Half-Silence:  Incorporating Female Sexual Violence Narratives into Public Histories of the 1971 Bangladeshi Genocide. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/hh138vj1618. https://doi.org/10.25740/hh138vj1618.",
